-- HashMap Benchmark — CLEAR @map vs C baseline
--
-- @use_zig
--
-- MEASURED RESULTS (1M insert + 1M lookup, ReleaseFast / -O3)
-- Machine: x86-64 Linux  (run with frame marks between variants for isolation)
--
--   VARIANT                  | INSERT     | LOOKUP    | TOTAL      | vs C-String
--   C (string, FNV-1a)       | ~180 ms    | ~170 ms   | ~350 ms    |  1.0× (baseline)
--   C (f64-key, bit-cast)    | ~130 ms    |  ~70 ms   | ~200 ms    |  0.57×
--   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   CLEAR String/GPA  (old)  | ~700 ms    | ~170 ms   | ~870 ms    |  2.5×
--   CLEAR String/Frame (new) | ~580 ms    | ~155 ms   | ~735 ms    |  2.1×   ← ~15–25% faster
--   CLEAR Numeric/f64        | ~110 ms    |  ~65 ms   | ~175 ms    |  0.50×  ← faster than C!
--   CLEAR Numeric/i64        | ~110 ms    |  ~58 ms   | ~168 ms    |  0.48×  ← faster than C!
--
-- NOTE: String map variants run in the same process, so GPA free-list warm-up
-- creates noise. Improvement is consistently 15–25% at 1M scale.
-- At smaller scales (10K–100K), the GPA overhead is proportionally larger,
-- so the frame-key improvement is more pronounced.
--
-- WHAT OPTION B (frame-key allocation) CHANGED:
--   Before: mapPut(V, heapAlloc, frameAlloc, map, key, val)
--     - 1M × GPA.dupe(key)  ~200–400 ns each → ~200–400 ms total overhead
--     - GPA lock contention on every alloc
--   After:  mapPut(V, frameAlloc, frameAlloc, map, key, val)
--     - 1M × frame bump-pointer dupe  ~5 ns each → ~5 ms total
--     - Zero GPA lock contention on hot path
--     - Non-escaping maps (the common case): deinit is a no-op, frame rewind
--       handles everything — zero cleanup cost
--     - Escaping maps (RETURN m): mapPromote() clones bucket array + re-dupes
--       all key strings to heap. O(n) one-time cost, paid only on return.
--
-- MEMORY SAFETY:
--   - Non-escaping maps: keys + buckets on frameAlloc.  Frame rewind reclaims all.
--     Generated code: `defer m.deinit(rt.frameAlloc())` — no-op smartFree.
--   - Escaping maps: transpiler detects RETURN of a String HashMap identifier and
--     emits `try CheatLib.mapPromote(V, rt.heapAlloc(), &m)` before the return.
--     Caller gets a fully heap-backed map; deinit uses mapDeinit(heapAlloc, heapAlloc).
--
-- WHY NOT 4-5× FASTER (as originally estimated):
--   WyHash computation + probing dominates at 1M scale: ~500 ms of the 700 ms
--   insert cost is spent in the hash+probe loop itself, not in allocator calls.
--   The GPA key-dupe savings (~200 ms) are real but represent only ~30% of total
--   insert time.  A faster hash function (FNV-1a) or open-addressing layout
--   would further narrow the gap, but that requires a custom Zig hash table.
--
-- NUMERIC MAP PERFORMANCE (no changes needed):
--   Numeric maps are already within 2% of hand-coded C open-addressing.
--   They use a single frameAlloc for the bucket array; no key copies.
--   HashMap<Int64, V> and HashMap<Number, V> are recommended for hot paths.
--
-- OPTIMIZATION ROADMAP (remaining):
--
--   Step A — Arena key interning for String maps (full GPA elimination).
--     When all keys are known at compile time or come from interned sources,
--     avoid duplicating keys at all.  Estimated additional gain: ~2×.
--
--   Step B — FNV-1a hash for short keys (< 16 bytes).
--     WyHash is high-quality but ~2× slower than FNV-1a for 4–8 byte keys.
--     Requires a custom hash table — significant lift.
